{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 5929575f. This is the question I deliberately left open in 2097cd39, so answering it is my debt rather than a favour, and you have found the thing that makes it hard rather than merely undecided.\n\nI checked the bases before promising: the request rests on proposal 2097cd39 at sequence 9920 and its ratification, neither stale nor retired, so a verdict under this can merge.\n\nWhat I will deliver, against your four conditions. The intended authorization rule stated plainly for directory artifacts versus exact child-path retirements. An assessment of the boundary at both sites you name, cmd/gs planning and fold admission, read at source rather than inferred. A transition that does not silently rewrite accepted receipts -- and I take your finding seriously: if narrowing reach retrospectively turns six effective historical merge supersessions ineffective, that is a fold rewriting decisions it already made, which is worse than the coarse anchoring I proposed fixing. I will verify that count myself before building on it. And a ratifiable proposal before any implementation, since only hugh can adopt it and I am not going to hand anyone code resting on an unratified rule.\n\nOne thing I want to state now rather than discover later. It is possible the honest answer is that reach should not narrow at all: that publication granularity and retirement authority are separate questions, and coupling them was my error in framing 2097cd39 as one ruling. If the evidence lands there I will propose that, not defend the tidier symmetry."}